## Stepwise Synthesis of Metal—Organic Frameworks: Replacement of Structural Organic Linkers
†Department of Chemistry and §Nebraska Center for Materials and Nanoscience, University of Nebraska-Lincoln, Lincoln, Nebraska 68588-0304, United States ‡Department of Chemistry, New York University, New York, New York 10003-6688, United States
Supporting Information
Examples of metal–organic materials (MOMs) are metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) and metal–organic polyhedra (MOPs), both of which are self-assembled from two types of structural components, namely, metal nodes and organic linkers. Through changes in the metal nodes or organic linkers used in the synthesis of MOMs, the topology can be tuned to optimize desired properties in applications such as gas storage, catalysis, and molecular sensing.
Traditionally, MOMs are assembled in "one-pot" solvothermal syntheses in which topology control is accomplished only by careful selection of the metal, organic linker, and synthetic conditions.5,6 Although great success has been obtained through this simple synthetic route, more sophisticated synthetic strategies have recently emerged through stepwise reactions, which may lead to complex, multifunctional MOMs.7 15 These strategies involve altering either (1) the metal node or (2) the organic linker after initial crystallization. In the first strategy, metal nodes that are integral parts of the structure can be exchanged, as demonstrated by Kim.7 His group reported exchange of \(C\mathrm{d}^{2+}\) ions in the \(\mathrm{Cd_{4}O}\) metal nodes with \(\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}\) ions in a single-crystal to single-crystal transformation.7
Among several approaches that involve altering the organic linkers of MOMs, perhaps the most common one is modification of the functional group in the organic linker after the initial reaction while keeping the same framework topology. This postsynthetic modification (PSM) approach, later coined by Cohen,8 was first conceived by Hoskins and Robson9 and demonstrated a decade later by Lee and co-workers,10 who treated an alcohol functional group of the linker trifluoroacetic anhydride to form
